welcome back to cbs mornings it's now  been a year since the last u.s troops  left afghanistan after 20 years of war  but even though the fighting has ended  there are still outbreaks of violence  including a suicide bomb attack outside  the russian embassy in kabul this  morning at least three people were  killed including two embassy staff our  mts taeb recently returned to the  country and visited a village that was  caught in the crossfire during the war  he met some of the afghans who are  struggling to recover from the conflict  the streets of wardak province are  bustling again less than an hour's drive  from kabul this taliban stronghold saw  little of the progress and freedoms the  capital did during the two decades of  america's longest war  trapped in the crossfire were villages  like this one dagbagri which today looks  like ancient ruins but what happened  here is a pain still felt by those who  once called it home  it's hard to imagine just 20 years ago  at the start of the war this was a  thriving village full of families but it  quickly became a front line between the  taliban and u.s forces  the village was destroyed  and everyone left  except for marshall the only remaining  resident  the fighting here must have been so  intense for such devastation  oh darcy that was jungkook  yes the fighting was very intense here  he says bullets hit every house u.s  tanks would stop on the main road and  shoot at our houses  in the bitter battles between the  thalabon and us forces ordinary families  were all too often caught in the  fighting or accused of supporting the  taliban the harsh tactics used by some  u.s forces and their afghan allies in  raids across rural afghanistan  are well documented the killings as well  as a lack of accountability drove many  people living in these areas to support  the taliban in a village nestled in this  valley menagedin says he was arrested in  a night raid by u.s forces 12 years ago  he tells us three of his sons were shot  dead as they slept we can't confirm his  account but it's been consistent down  the years and american raids were common  in this area  what did they ask you what did they say  to you  when the americans arrested me that  night they asked where the taliban were  he says i told them i had no connections  with them i asked if they had any proof  that i had met the taliban and they had  no answer  so basically your sons were killed  because of a mistake they  came to the wrong house  when they couldn't prove it they started  asking me why i didn't support the  government more he says i kept asking  how should i cooperate more  who do you blame for your son's killing  america  we blame americans he says why did they  kill innocent people  by 2015 u.s forces left bordak to the  afghan army this abandoned base now  flies the taliban flag after its  takeover one year ago a remnant of the  recent past that still haunts those who  call this area home for cbs morning's  impiaz taya wardak province afghanistan  powerful reporting by mts taeyab
 

